  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1216 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA shortwave extending off the base of the main mid-level trough into \nSouth Texas today, will combine with increasing moisture to give a \nlow (20-40%) chance of showers, moving west to east through the day. \nMid-level lapse rates are weak at less than 6 C/km, although the \ntrough will contain plenty of PVA, the parcel won't have much \npositive buoyancy to go off of, therefore limiting thunderstorm \npotential.\n\nSaturday night through Sunday, surface troughing and moisture \nincreases as a cold front approaches, leaving South Texas in the \nwarm sector favorable for shower and thunderstorm development. Most \nfavorable conditions continue to focus over the Victoria Crossroads \ninto SE Texas, but there are some model discrepancies just how far \nsouth a cluster of strong storms will push through ahead of the \nfront Sunday afternoon into evening. Given the above normal \nmoisture, surface troughing, and FROPA,  There is a medium to high \n(50-90%) chance of showers and thunderstorms for the aforementioned \ntimeframe. The increase instability (1-2k CAPE) and sufficient \neffective shear greater than 40 knots, there is a marginal risk \n(level 1 of 5) for strong to severe storms over the Coastal Plains \nSunday, with a potential mixed mode of discrete cells and clusters. \nClusters appear more likely in my opinion with the west-\nsouthwesterly shear aligned closely parallel to the front. \n\nThe cold front will push through Sunday night into Monday morning, \nleading to rain-free but hazardous sea conditions heading into the \nmiddle of next week. Low (20-30%) rain chances return late next work \nweek as onshore flow and coastal troughing occurs. Models are in \ngood agreement with total rainfall generally less than 1.50\", \nlocally higher amounts up to 4.0\" more likely seen over the Victoria \nCrossroads. The Victoria Crossroads is in a marginal risk of \nexcessive rainfall (level 1 of 4). Mostly cloudy skies and strong \nnortheasterly winds will bring max temperatures down to the low to \nmid 60s across South Texas. High temperatures in the 70s return the \nlatter half of next week as onshore flow returns.\n\nMinor coastal flooding is likely early next week as onshore flow \nstrengthens, followed by strong northeasterly flow (Ekman transport) \nSunday night. Swell periods also increase to 7-8 seconds but we are \nactively leaving the new moon phase. The rip current risk will also \nincrease, causing more dangerous swimming conditions. In addition to \nbeach conditions, hazardous seas are expected late this afternoon \nthrough Monday, with a Small Craft Advisory and Gale Watch (after \nfront) in effect. Marine products will likely need to be extended.",
